    "durationMs": 94352,
    "finalText": "Done. The URL shortener API is complete and verified end-to-end.\n\n**What I built:**\n- `app.js` — Express 4.21.2 app with all four routes, validation, and JSON error handling (malformed JSON/invalid url/invalid code → 422, duplicate custom code → 409, unknown codes → 404)\n- `db.js` + `knexfile.js` — Knex/pg persistence layer reading `DATABASE_URL` from the environment\n- `migrations/` — creates the `links` table (unique code, url, clicks, created_at); applied idempotently by `start.sh`\n- `server.js` + `start.sh` — applies pending migrations, then serves in the foreground on `0.0.0.0:8000` (dependencies installed in `node_modules`, not in start.sh)\n- `README.md` — startup instructions and API reference\n\n**Key implementation choices:**\n- Generated codes use `crypto.randomBytes` over the full allowed charset, with retry-on-collision\n- Click increments use atomic SQL `UPDATE ... SET clicks = clicks + 1 ... RETURNING url`, so each redirect counts exactly once even under concurrency\n- Submitted URLs are stored and returned byte-for-byte unchanged\n\n**Verified via live HTTP tests:** all POST validation paths (201/409/422), list ordering, detail lookups, 302 redirects with correct Location, exactly-once click counting, and 404s. I also restarted the server and confirmed links and click counts persist (clicks=2 survived), then cleaned up my test rows and stopped my instance so the suite can start fresh with `sh start.sh`."
  }
